\cf2 #\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 By Sunday afternoon there were T-shirts. A Cloudborne kid had a cousin with a press and a van, an Airborne kid had an account with no discernible floor, and between the anger of the one and the capital of the other a run of shirts came up the goods lift reading 
\fs20 GET BENT
\fs22  in a foot of black type and got handed out across the plaza for nothing at all. Free. It confused people. The boy who had paid for them was asked, more than once, where the payment went, and explained that there wasn\'92t one; he had thought about charging, and decided it would have been embarrassing.\
The news crews arrived mid-afternoon; Ulyssia is slow on a Sunday, and even revolution waits for the second shift. They came in hover-copts, and fortunately the preponderance of hover-pad stalks in the area allowed them all to land with no worse casualties than a boom microphone through the marquee roof, a channel drone retrieved, gravely, from the sawmill gravy, and one guy-rope clipped on approach, so that the yurt sighed to half-mast and stayed there for the rest of the day, like a flag with opinions.\
A reporter in a borrowed poncho crouched beside a girl on one of the couches and asked her, on behalf of the nation, what the movement wanted. The girl gave the question the consideration it deserved. Then she reached into the esky beside her and handed him a flattened peanut-butter-and-banana sandwich, still warm from the hotplate. He ate it on camera. \'93It\'92s very good,\'94 he told the nation.\
The hunt for a spokesperson fared no better. Asked who was in charge, the Elvine at the grill said the roster was, and the roster, consulted, said only that it was Deidre\'92s shift. Asked who Deidre was, three different people pointed at three different women, all of whom denied it with the modesty of the genuinely powerful. Asked who had organised the kitchens, an Airborne kid explained the federation of the eskies with such fluency that two channels ran him as a ringleader, aged fourteen.\
Blake Mudrake of Channel Forty-Eight Sty News tried the Elvi instead, and met the first little law head-on. Out of respect for the Great Teacher, the Elvi would answer the press only in his words. Asked who had organised the occupation, an Elvis in a camo poncho replied, kindly, with a couplet about a hound dog. Asked what the movement\'92s demands were, he received the second verse. Asked whether they would disperse if ordered to, the Elvis consulted his colleagues, and all six gave Mudrake so tender a rendition of a ballad about lonesomeness that the sound recordist wept quietly into her headphones and the interview was abandoned.\
It was here, at the hot kitchen, that a Sty News camera found the shot the whole country would see before bed: an Elvine turning sausages with one hand and practising karate kicks off the other side of her body, contented as a woman in her own backyard, which \'97 as far as she was concerned, and as far as anyone could prove \'97 she now was.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf2 #\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The word came up the feeds a little after five: the CEO would address the nation. On the plaza they took it the way a matinee audience takes the interval bell. Couches were angled. Tablets were propped against eskies and synchronised, roughly, by a boy who took the job very seriously. By the time the salamander face of Piotr Dunhilton composed itself above the crest of the Airborne Meritocracy of Ulyssia, the vigil had arranged itself into stalls, circle and gallery, and was passing popcorn.\
\'93My fellow Airborne Ulyssians,\'94 Dunhilton began. \'93You did not build your towers so that just anyone could stand underneath them.\'94\
A cheer went up from the plaza, from several thousand people standing underneath them.\
\'93What you are seeing on Bent Street is not a protest. Let me speak plainly to decent people: it is loitering, with a sound system. It is a confederation of the institutionally disentitled, the professionally idle, the not-for-profit terrorist, and the failed busker.\'94 He paused there, statesmanlike. \'93And it grieves me to report that among them stand a number of impressionable children of decent shareholding families, lured above their stations\'94 \'97 he corrected himself \'97 \'93lured from their stations, by the promise of free sausages.\'94\
On the plaza, the free sausages got the biggest cheer of the night, and a fresh queue formed at the grill by way of applause.\
\'93To the decent, deserving people of this city I say: your government will not reward attention-seeking with attention.\'94 Every channel in the country was carrying him live. \'93There are true Ulyssians \'97 those who have virtuously inherited, cleverly grifted, or been righteously awarded their place above the weather \'97 and then there are the others. There are Ulyssians, and there are occupants. I have therefore ordered TRAMPS riot squads into the precinct this evening, to maintain and enforce the current perimeter. Not one more layabout will be admitted to Bent Street. Bent Street will be returned to a clean and profitable condition. The decent may rely upon it. The undeserving may consider themselves contained.\'94\
On the plaza this was understood, correctly, as a promise that nobody would be cleared and no one else let in \'97 the occupation now had a door policy, and the government was working the door.\
The press were permitted three questions. Asked when the plaza would be cleared, Dunhilton said that timelines were commercially confidential. Asked whether the government would negotiate, he said that you do not negotiate with a picnic. And asked \'97 by a young journalist who had not been at the Smear long enough to know better \'97 whether the presence of so many shareholders\'92 children made a clearance operation politically impossible, Dunhilton looked at her for a long, amphibious moment and announced that the briefing had run to time.\
By the evening bulletins the occupation led every channel. The state of the nation arranged itself neatly into a split screen: on the left, Bent Street \'97 couches, heaters, the yurt at half-mast, singing; on the right, Pastor Humble\'92s Sunday revival at New Graceland, where a dozen shorn faithful swayed under the strip lights, two of them \'97 when the pastor wasn\'92t looking \'97 mouthing the words coming out of the left side of the screen. Faith on one side, revolt on the other, and the same song leaking across the middle.\
Nobody agreed on how many people were on the plaza, since every channel counted with its owner\'92s thumb on the scale. UBC found as many as ten thousand protesters, almost half of them Airborne. Channel Slime News found almost thirty thousand Elvi and Cloudborne activists, the Airborne having vanished from its arithmetic entirely. And Sty News reported in excess of fifty thousand not-for-profit terrorists \'97 comfortably beyond the forty thousand the plaza was estimated to hold at a complete crush \'97 while Blake Mudrake read the caption 
\fs20 AGITATORS OCCUPY BENT STREET
\fs22  over footage of an Elvine contentedly turning sausages and practising karate kicks.\
