\cf2 The riot squads came up at dusk, in goods lifts, eight at a time, and formed their line where the plaza met the street \'97 shields locked, visors down, enforcing the current perimeter with everything TRAMPS training could bring to bear against a crowd that showed no inclination to go anywhere near it. An Elvine walked the line at supper time offering sausages in bread. The sergeant declined. He declined again at the second pass. On the third he accepted one, for evidentiary purposes, and ate the evidence where he stood.\
The second night came on warmer than the first. The heaters ticked alight along the couches, the coals settled in the barbecues, and the plaza drew in around its fires the way it had drawn in around its kitchens, which is to say without anybody deciding anything.\
The other Elvi currency was story, and it got spent around the barbecue as the second day wore on. An Elvine with the trailer at her back and a queue in front of her told the tale of the Dawkins to a rapt semicircle of Airborne kids \'97 the one long blind accident, the survival of the fittest, the Holy Momma who bore the first of them in a shotgun house with the power off \'97 and the kids listened, chins on knees, to a doctrine of natural selection recited to several hundred people who had never once been selected against, and found it, they said, incredibly deep. Nobody was converted. It was not that kind of night. The stories went round the way stories go round a fire, because a fire is where a people keeps the things it cannot afford to lose, and these three peoples were, for one weekend only, keeping theirs in the same place.\
High in the glass of TRAMP Tower, in a lit window a long way above the couches, a night guard stood looking down at the fires. A girl on a couch saw him, and waved up. The guard checked the empty lobby behind him, forgot eleven years of training, and waved back \'97 and the cheer that went up the glass was the warmest sound the precinct had ever had aimed at it. He stepped away from the window at once. He stayed near it, though, for most of his shift.\
On the couches they passed back what was left of the marshmallows, which had been ordered twice, because by the second night nobody could remember what was still missing and it had seemed safest to repeat the essentials. The singing kept on at the buildings, lower now, the kind of singing that is mostly for the singers. And at the eastern end of the plaza the first tarp still hung where the lad had strung it, 
\fs20 OCCUPY BENT STREET
\fs22  in dripping black; but by then it read less like a slogan than a forwarding address.\
